WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:10.410
It's very important given the nature

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:13.430
of the climate crisis right
now for the general public

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:15.950
to have an understanding
of what progress is

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:18.020
or is not actually happening.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:19.910
And I think many readers right now

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:22.170
they'll see these hundreds
and hundreds of companies

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:25.963
making these big, bold climate claims.

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:33.760
And they would be forgiven to think that,

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:35.200
we're well on our way to meeting

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.610
the emission reduction
trajectory we need to be on

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:40.300
and the reality of the matter is

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.418
we're just not on that trajectory.

00:00:42.418 --> 00:00:46.720
And a key reason for
this sort of disconnect

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:49.230
is the use of offsets that aren't doing

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:50.630
what they claim to be doing.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:55.350
We have a massive potential
for sort of willful blindness.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:57.630
We can convince ourselves of things

00:00:57.630 --> 00:00:59.800
regardless of what the facts are.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.559
And so that's what tends
to happen in these cases.

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:04.490
It's not that anybody is setting out

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:06.700
to commit climate fraud.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.980
They of walk themselves into a situation

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:11.850
and they convince themselves of one thing

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:13.570
and that allows them
to convince themselves

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:14.760
of another thing.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.140
But at the end of the day
they're doing something

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:17.490
that makes no sense at all.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.270
My name is Ben Elgin.

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:31.920
I wrote the story about how
the billion dollar market

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:34.060
for carbon offsets is setting back

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:35.793
the fight against climate change.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:39.650
So one of the challenges in
reporting on carbon offsets

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:41.410
and trying to figure out what's going on

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:43.820
is really finding good experts

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:46.730
who have been steeped in this marketplace

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:49.170
and are willing to candidly talk about it.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:50.410
One of the best people

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:52.287
for this is a fellow named Mark Trexler

00:01:52.287 --> 00:01:55.850
and I've been talking to
him for more than a decade.

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:57.090
My name is Mark Trexler.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:00.550
I've worked in climate
change for the last 30 years.

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:02.000
I was actually hired by

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.920
the World Resources
Institute in Washington

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.920
to work on the first carbon
offset project in 1988.

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:11.430
In terms of defining a carbon offset

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:14.570
one of the beauties so to
speak of greenhouse gases,

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:17.750
sort of the silver lining
of greenhouse gases.

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:21.080
When you put up a molecule
of CO2 into the atmosphere

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:25.260
it can be anywhere in
the planet in seven days.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:29.380
And therefore, if there's an
option to reduce emissions

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:31.170
on the other side of the planet

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:33.130
and you can do that very cheaply

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:36.520
as compared to doing it in
your factory for example,

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:39.960
then why not pay that person, that factory

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:43.120
on the other side of the planet
to reduce their emissions

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:47.480
then you are in a sense
offsetting your emissions

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:49.000
and the atmosphere doesn't

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.433
really see your emissions anymore.

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:56.160
So carbon offsets have been
around for quite some time

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:57.460
and there were actually key

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:00.810
with these international
climate agreements

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:03.590
such as the Kyoto Protocol, which mandated

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:05.280
the wealthiest countries reduce

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.000
their greenhouse gas emissions.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:07.930
The treaty was signed

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:10.950
on the 11th of December, 1997.

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:11.783
Decided.

00:03:12.942 --> 00:03:16.700
The business community in
general saw carbon offsets

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:21.480
as a huge opportunity to
comply with whatever policies

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.470
and measures might be coming down the pike

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:26.820
on climate change, much
more cost-effectively.

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:28.700
We've seen a huge surge

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:32.080
in companies making big proclamations

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:34.290
about how they're going carbon neutral

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:37.340
or zeroing out all of
their carbon emissions.

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:38.470
We're going to reduce

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:40.980
our carbon footprint to net zero.

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:44.020
Unilever is committing
to net zero emissions.

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:47.940
We can achieve a net zero emissions world.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:50.700
We began looking at these corporate claims

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.030
of have dramatically reduced emissions.

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:56.060
And we noticed so many of these companies

00:03:56.060 --> 00:04:01.040
were using carbon offsets
to achieve these aims.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:02.460
And as we began to look

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:05.170
at the carbon offset projects themselves,

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:08.480
what we found is that the
environmental benefits claimed

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:12.403
by these companies are not
actually what they seem to be.

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:19.640
And as we sort of saw and spotted a number

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:22.270
of these very aggressive
carbon offset projects

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:24.940
which appeared to be
generating inflated credits

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:27.870
or bogus credits, we saw a number of them

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:30.083
were associated with
The Nature Conservancy.

00:04:32.728 --> 00:04:33.561
And this surprised us.

00:04:33.561 --> 00:04:35.250
The Nature Conservancy is the world's

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:37.040
biggest environmental group.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:40.590
They have about a billion
dollars a year in revenues

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:42.883
and they've been around for seven decades.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:47.593
They've preserved about
125 million acres of land.

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:51.210
And they've done so many
good works over the years.

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:52.530
And they've been very strong

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:54.730
in terms of working
with corporate partners.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:58.250
The Nature Conservancy has
taken a different approach,

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:00.580
a very pragmatic and
pro-business approach.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:03.080
They will not go out and
criticize a big polluter

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:05.423
but they're very happy to work with them.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:08.570
Some might say that
well that's selling out

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:09.640
but others would say,

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:12.720
and those at The Nature
Conservancy would say,

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:14.800
these companies have deep pockets,

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.890
they can help fund
important preservation work

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:19.940
and we have absolutely no problem working

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:21.810
with them on this stuff.

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:26.690
So companies are very
warm and they've partnered

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:30.140
with The Nature
Conservancy for many years.

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:32.990
And yeah, it definitely lends a sheen

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:35.043
of credibility to these projects.

00:05:36.787 --> 00:05:38.004
Here are 20 reasons

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:40.078
to visit the Disney World this year.

00:05:40.078 --> 00:05:43.703
One, two, three,

00:05:43.703 --> 00:05:45.140
four, five, six, seven.

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:46.790
If you look at for instance Disney

00:05:46.790 --> 00:05:50.000
that's a sprawling
company with theme parks

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:54.010
and cruise lines that burn up diesel fuel.

00:05:54.010 --> 00:05:55.190
They have office buildings

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:58.640
and television studios
that consume electricity.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:01.090
So these companies are
trying to make major,

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:02.920
major reductions in
their carbon footprint,

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:04.680
but it's very difficult to change

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.673
these fundamental ways
their businesses operate.

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:12.880
So Disney has been a big buyer

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:15.720
of carbon offsets that are orchestrated

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:17.990
by The Nature Conservancy.

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:19.430
One of the projects that they buy

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:22.460
from is called Pennsylvania Ridges

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:23.650
and that's a plot of land,

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:26.250
most of the land is
actually about a three-hour

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:28.083
drive outside of Philadelphia.

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:32.740
Interestingly, this
land, 3000 of these acres

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:34.280
was actually acquired

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:37.703
by The Nature Conservancy
in the late 1990s.

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:40.450
This land was imminently threatened.

00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:43.970
There was a pending logging
contract on this land.

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:46.620
So they do what they've done so well

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:49.040
for all of these years,
which is they go out,

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:51.040
they raise money from
local philanthropists

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:53.100
and they go buy this land to protect it

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:54.743
from developers and loggers.

00:06:55.610 --> 00:06:58.250
And at the time, according
to an archived version

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:00.493
of The Nature Conservancy's website,

00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:04.250
when they were proudly
announcing this deal, they said,

00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:07.850
these threats have been abated
because of this acquisition

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:09.480
by The Nature Conservancy.

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:12.510
So it's really interesting
now, fast forward two decades

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:13.770
to earlier this year

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:16.370
when they put forward this carbon project.

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:18.420
And if you look through it, this land

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:20.860
is a key part of that carbon project.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:23.040
And what they're now saying is this land

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:26.260
is now once again imminently threatened,

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:30.710
72% of the trees are gonna
be cut within five years.

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:32.920
Now this is just astounding
because this land

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:34.840
is owned by The Nature Conservancy.

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:37.130
They do not clear-cut
land that they've bought

00:07:37.130 --> 00:07:39.503
through philanthropic donations.

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:42.570
So this scenario that they've put forward

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:46.170
to get carbon revenues
is not really plausible.

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:48.840
And what that means is when
you have Disney coming in

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:53.150
and buying these credits,
when it eventually lowers

00:07:53.150 --> 00:07:56.280
its carbon footprint,
because of these credits,

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:59.573
well that won't be a credible
or truthful claim by Disney.

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:08.210
So this is an organization
that truly understands

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:11.320
the threat of the issue of climate change

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:15.010
and is wanting to do its
part to address this.

00:08:15.010 --> 00:08:17.010
However in this situation it just appears

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:19.980
their drive which they've had for decades

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:22.810
to increase the amount of
revenues that it can use

00:08:22.810 --> 00:08:24.563
for land preservation work.

00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:29.610
Nature Conservancy and many
others see that carbon credits

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:32.410
as a pot of money that they can tap into

00:08:32.410 --> 00:08:36.800
to further their own benevolent aims.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:40.730
But unfortunately what this
has resulted in is claims

00:08:40.730 --> 00:08:43.713
of climate progress that
just aren't really happening.

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:49.730
So The Nature Conservancy
defends vigorously

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:54.160
these deals and they and
others will say, look,

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:56.320
our projects are just following the rules

00:08:56.320 --> 00:08:57.883
that these registry set up.

00:08:59.090 --> 00:09:00.920
So some would also put some of the blame

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:02.260
on the registries themselves

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:04.070
because they're setting up the rules

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:08.043
which allow these vapid
projects to get up and sold.

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:12.110
So we've reached out
to all of the companies

00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:14.630
we mentioned in this
story, including Disney,

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:16.560
they didn't want to be
interviewed for this story

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:19.690
but they all told us that, look,

00:09:19.690 --> 00:09:21.950
we have to rely on the experts out there.

00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:25.080
And those experts they say
are these carbon registries

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:26.740
that have been set up.

00:09:26.740 --> 00:09:29.420
Most of these nature Conservancy deals

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:32.530
are set up and sold on this exchange,

00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:35.460
it's called the American Carbon Registry.

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:37.700
Now it's the oldest of these registries

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:41.550
that allow voluntary
carbon credits to be sold.

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:44.200
They've been around since 1996.

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:47.950
What these offset registries
do is they set up rules

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:51.890
and any carbon offset project
has to follow those rules,

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:54.420
but there's this tension
that's always there.

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:58.700
If the rules are too strict,
then it's gonna be very hard

00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:01.110
for carbon offset projects
to get up and running.

00:10:01.110 --> 00:10:03.290
So there's this balance going on

00:10:03.290 --> 00:10:06.620
where the registries want
rules that attract projects

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:08.420
but they don't want it to be so strict

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:12.453
where it will disincentivize
projects from getting listed.

00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:19.070
One of the challenges with
designing carbon offset systems

00:10:19.070 --> 00:10:22.830
is that there are normally
two people at the table.

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:24.770
You have buyers and you have sellers.

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:27.440
And to some extent you have policymakers,

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:30.320
but the buyers and the
sellers are really the ones

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:33.200
with the expertise, with the incentive

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:35.010
to really participate hard,

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:36.483
make a lot of things happen.

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:39.470
And the challenge there is,

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:42.520
is that both of them sort
of want the same thing

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:44.220
or they're both happy with the same thing.

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:48.110
They're happy with low cost
offsets and a lot of them,

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:50.310
because that works for
their business model.

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:55.990
You end up though with
nobody really representing

00:10:55.990 --> 00:10:58.000
the atmosphere, so to speak,

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:00.750
nobody's really representing the climate

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:02.040
in those discussions.

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:06.000
And so the policies end up
biasing the whole system

00:11:07.190 --> 00:11:10.600
and you end up in sort
of a race to the bottom,

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:12.300
and that's what we've seen happen.

00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:17.003
When it comes specifically to
the topic of carbon offsets,

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:20.800
I am a fan of carbon offsets

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:23.291
in the sense that they can
help mitigate climate change,

00:11:23.291 --> 00:11:24.683
they absolutely can,

00:11:24.683 --> 00:11:28.030
but if we're not going to do it correctly

00:11:28.030 --> 00:11:31.130
in a way that actually does
help mitigate climate change,

00:11:31.130 --> 00:11:33.210
then it's nothing but a distraction.

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:36.840
And after 30 years you
have to start wondering,

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:38.570
is it really just a distraction?

00:11:38.570 --> 00:11:41.320
And that's really unfortunate
that that's where we are.

00:11:43.750 --> 00:11:48.390
Ultimately what needs to
happen is systemic change.

00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:50.460
We're sort of nibbling around the edges

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:52.570
with some of these different things.

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:55.590
We need change how we produce energy.

00:11:55.590 --> 00:11:58.870
We need to change how
we transport ourselves.

00:11:58.870 --> 00:12:02.950
We need to change what and how we eat.

00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:06.350
We need systemic change associated

00:12:06.350 --> 00:12:09.473
with transitioning to
a low carbon economy.

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:13.080
A lot of what we're doing today

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:14.990
is not promoting systemic change,

00:12:14.990 --> 00:12:19.440
it's sort of trying to
stick your thumb in the dyke

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:21.273
to preserve the current system.

00:12:22.587 --> 00:12:26.100
And at the end of the day
that can't really work.

